exec(open(__file__.replace('physical-main.py','repair.py')).read().split("step('lem-cm-lattice")[0])
id='thm-positive-measure-analytic-kam-family'
replace(id,'Let $n\\ge2$',r'Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the Lebesgue-measure frequency-set supplier. Let $n\ge2$')
replace(id,'deps: [','deps: [def-countable-choice, ')
replace(id,'dependency_roles:\n','dependency_roles:\n  def-countable-choice: mathematical-premise\n')
replace(id,'a compact cube $C^*$ with','a compact cube $C^*$ with positive side length and')
replace(id,r'$$\Omega_{\alpha,\tau}:=',r'$$\Omega_{\alpha,\tau}:=') if False else None
replace(id,r'\Psi_\infty(0,\vartheta,\nu)=(b(\nu)+\Phi_I(0,\vartheta,\nu),\vartheta+w(\vartheta,\nu))',r'\Psi_\infty(0,\vartheta,\nu)=(b(\nu)+\Phi_I(0,\vartheta,\nu),\vartheta+w(\vartheta,\nu))') if False else None
replace(id,r'$|\Phi_I(0,\vartheta,\nu)|\le2CrE_0$ and $|w(\vartheta,\nu)|\le2C\sigma_0E_0$',r'$|\Phi_I(0,\vartheta,\nu)|\le2C_{\mathrm{phys}}rE_0$ and $|w(\vartheta,\nu)|\le2C_{\mathrm{phys}}\sigma_0E_0$')
replace(id,'the family $\\nu\\mapsto\\Psi_\\infty(0,\\vartheta,\\nu)$ is Lipschitz on $\\Omega_{\\alpha,\\tau}$ with the constant of [[lem-kam-composed-map-and-derivative-convergence]] clause 3.',r'the total action correction $\Phi_I$ includes the frequency-retraction change of its centre. Here $C_{\mathrm{phys}}$ is a fixed finite constant depending on the stated analytic data, including a bound for $Db$ near $C^*$. The family $\nu\mapsto\Psi_\infty(0,\vartheta,\nu)$ is Lipschitz on $\Omega_{\alpha,\tau}$ with a finite constant at most $C_{\mathrm{phys}}[1+(r+\sigma_0)E_0/h_0]$, where $h_0=\alpha/(2K_0^{\tau+1})$ is the initial frequency width chosen in the proof. The constant term retains the variation of the unperturbed centre $b(\nu)$.')
p=ROOT/'items'/(id+'.md');s=p.read_text();a=s.index('[F2]');b=s.index('\n\n',a)
s=s[:a]+r'[F2] The one-step and iteration suppliers [[lem-kam-affine-taylor-fourier-truncation]], [[lem-truncated-homological-equation-with-conservative-loss]], [[lem-kam-generator-flow-and-remainder-bound]], [[lem-frequency-reparametrization-by-contraction]] and [[lem-kam-parameter-sequences-satisfy-all-step-hypotheses]] provide the shrinking-domain remainder, inverse frequency retractions and the complete parameter estimates with $h_j=h_04^{-\mu j}$, angular loss $8\sigma_j$, and $E_j\le4^{-(\mu+1)j}E_0$. The latter lemma gives simultaneous initial feasibility uniformly for $0<\alpha\le1$.'+s[b:]
start=s.index('## Proof\n');end=s.index('\n## Remarks',start)
proof=r'''## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct; choose the actual iteration widths, retain the original-parameter map, transfer the invariant limit to the physical Hamiltonian, and sharpen its action displacement by averaging the angular equation.

**Given:** The analytic data of the Statement and facts [F1]-[F6]. Write $\sigma:=\min(\sigma_0,s_0/32)$, so $\sigma\ge3\sigma_0/4$, and let $C,A,C_L,c_1,C_*$ be fixed constants sufficient for the one-step, sequence, composition and embedding suppliers.

1.1 Choose $h_{\mathrm{geom}}>0$ such that its closed frequency neighbourhood of $C^*$ is contained in $\Omega'$ and $b$ maps it into the complex tube $\operatorname{dist}_\infty(J,K)<r_0/2$. This is possible by compactness, continuity, and $b(C^*)\subseteq K$. Let $M_b$ be a finite bound for the operator norm of $Db$ on a slightly smaller such neighbourhood. Fix an initial error threshold $e_*>0$ below $\min(c_1^2,c_1,\sigma^2,1/64,4^{-2(\mu+1)}/C_*^2)$ and small enough for the initial feasibility assertion of [F2], the composition bounds $nAe_*\le1/2$, and the invariance smallness $Ce_*\le1/16$ after enlarging $C\ge A$. All these constants depend only on fixed data. Set $c>0$ no larger than $1$, $h_{\mathrm{geom}}/2$, $M_2r_0^2$, $e_*^2\sigma^{2\mu}/(4M_2)$, and the positive-measure threshold $|C^*|/[2C_{n,\tau}(\operatorname{diam}C^*)^{n-1}]$. For $0<\varepsilon\le c\alpha^2$, put $r=\sqrt{\varepsilon/M_2}$ and $E:=2\sqrt{\varepsilon M_2}/(\alpha\sigma^\mu)\le e_*$. Choose the explicit cutoff $K_0=\lceil\max(1,2L)/\sigma\rceil$ of the sequence lemma and $h_0=\alpha/(2K_0^{\tau+1})$. By [F2] its lower width condition holds once $e_*$ is sufficiently small; $\alpha<c\le1$ makes the upper cap one automatic. Also $h_0\le\alpha/2<h_{\mathrm{geom}}/4$. [given, F2, F5, choose, algebra]

1.2 For $\lambda\in O_{h_0}(\Omega_{\alpha,\tau})$ the base point $b(\lambda)$ is within $r_0/2$ of $K$, and the action segment $b(\lambda)+tI$, $|I|_\infty<r\le r_0$, remains in the Hessian-control tube. Hence [F4] applies and the function $G=h(b(\lambda)+I)-h(b(\lambda))-\lambda\cdot I+F(b(\lambda)+I,\vartheta)$ is holomorphic, periodic, real on real points and bounded by $2\varepsilon$. With $e_0(\lambda)=h(b(\lambda))$, the identity $H_0(I,\vartheta,\lambda):=e_0(\lambda)+\lambda\cdot I+G(I,\vartheta,\lambda)=H(b(\lambda)+I,\vartheta)$ is exact. The relative error for this perturbation is $E$ of step 1.1. The strict strip margin is valid because $\sigma\le s_0/32$. [step 1.1, F1, F4, algebra]

2.1 Apply [F2] inductively to $P_0=G$. At stage $j$, the parameter lemma verifies every one-step hypothesis, including $E_j\le c_1\eta_j$, the finite-cutoff divisor bound, the full remainder estimate on action radius $\eta_jr_j$, and the frequency shift bound $B_j=C\varepsilon_j/r_j\le h_{j+1}/(16n)$. The inverse retraction solves $\phi_j+v_j\circ\phi_j=\mathrm{id}$. Thus the transformed Hamiltonian is $H_{j+1}=e_{j+1}(\nu)+\nu\cdot I+P_{j+1}$ on the widths $r_{j+1}=\eta_jr_j$, $s_{j+1}=s_j-8\sigma_j$, $h_{j+1}$, with $|P_{j+1}|\le\varepsilon_{j+1}$ by the actual remainder budget $\varepsilon_{j+1}=C_*2^{-\mu}E_j\varepsilon_j\ge3CE_j\varepsilon_j$. The canonical phase map and parameter retraction form the extended step. Therefore the hypotheses of [[lem-kam-composed-map-and-derivative-convergence]] are met, with its exact identity $H_0(\Psi_j,\chi_j)=e_j+\nu\cdot I+P_j$. Its limits have the form $\Psi_\infty=(a,\vartheta+w)$ and $\lambda=\chi_\infty(\nu)$, with $|a|\le ArE$, $|w|\le A\sigma E$, $\|D_\vartheta w\|\le nAE\le1/2$, and $|\lambda-\nu|\le AB_0$. The last bound and the initial-width estimates keep the original parameter inside the fixed geometric neighbourhood. [step 1.2, F2, F3, algebra]

3.1 The invariance supplier [F3] applies to this extended identity and gives $X_{H_0}(\Psi_\infty;\chi_\infty(\nu))=D_\vartheta\Psi_\infty\,\nu$ and vanishing pullback of the canonical two-form. Translating the actions by the fixed vector $b(\chi_\infty(\nu))$ is canonical, and $H_0(z,\lambda)=H(b(\lambda)+I,\vartheta)$ by step 1.2. Consequently the physical lift $\Psi^{\mathrm{phys}}_\nu(\vartheta)=(b(\chi_\infty(\nu))+a(\vartheta,\nu),\vartheta+w(\vartheta,\nu))$ obeys $X_H(\Psi^{\mathrm{phys}}_\nu)=D_\vartheta\Psi^{\mathrm{phys}}_\nu\,\nu$ and has zero pullback of the two-form. Reality and angular equivariance follow from the real one-step construction. The lift descends to the quotient torus. To prove injectivity, for fixed real $\nu$ solve $\vartheta=q-w(\vartheta,\nu)$ on $\mathbb R^n$ by contraction with constant at most $1/2$; the bounded periodic correction gives a unique inverse for every $q$. Equivariance makes this inverse descend modulo $2\pi$. Hence equality of physical angular components forces equality of parameter angles on the torus; the physical phase map is injective and, by compactness of the torus, a homeomorphism onto its image. This establishes the invariant Lagrangian tori of clause 2, with motion obtained by evaluating the embedding at parameter phase $\vartheta_0+s\nu$. [step 1.2, step 2.1, F3, F4, F6, algebra]

4.1 We retain the stated action closeness about $b(\nu)$, not merely about $b(\chi_\infty(\nu))$. Fix a real label, write $\lambda=\chi_\infty(\nu)$, and average the angular component of the invariance identity over the original periodic parameter angles: since every derivative of the periodic correction has mean zero, $\nu-\lambda=\operatorname{mean}[Dh(b(\lambda)+a)-Dh(b(\lambda))]+\operatorname{mean}F_p(b(\lambda)+a,\vartheta+w)$. Polarization of the Hessian quadratic-form bound gives every Hessian entry bounded by $M_2$, so its induced maximum-norm bound is at most $nM_2$. The coarse displacement bounds already place the real action segment in a fixed inner tube; Cauchy's estimate on action discs of radius $r_0/4$ gives $|F_p|\le4\varepsilon/r_0$. Therefore $|\lambda-\nu|\le nM_2ArE+4\varepsilon/r_0$. Along the segment from $\nu$ to $\lambda$, $|b(\lambda)-b(\nu)|\le M_b|\lambda-\nu|$. Since $\varepsilon/(rE)=\alpha\sigma^\mu/2\le1/2$, the total physical action correction is at most $[A+M_b(nM_2A+2/r_0)]rE$. Finally $E\le(4/3)^\mu E_0$ for the $E_0$ in the Statement. Taking $C_{\mathrm{phys}}$ at least half this fixed coefficient times $(4/3)^\mu$ yields $|\Phi_I|\le2C_{\mathrm{phys}}rE_0$; the same enlargement gives $|w|\le2C_{\mathrm{phys}}\sigma_0E_0$. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F1, F4, F6, algebra]

4.2 The phase-limit Lipschitz estimate in the composition lemma is $2C_L(r+\sigma)E/h_0$, and its parameter limit has Lipschitz constant at most $1+C_LB_0/h_0$. The fixed neighbourhood of the compact cube may be chosen convex; the bound for $Db$ therefore gives the physical-centre Lipschitz constant at most $M_b(1+C_LB_0/h_0)$. Because $B_0=C\alpha E\sigma^\mu\le C\sigma E$, adding the phase and centre bounds and using $E\le(4/3)^\mu E_0$ gives the stated $C_{\mathrm{phys}}[1+(r+\sigma_0)E_0/h_0]$ after increasing the same fixed constant. The constant term is needed even when the perturbation tends to zero, because $b(\nu)$ varies. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 4.1, F3, algebra]

5.1 The resonance-slab estimate [F5] gives $|\Omega_{\alpha,\tau}|\ge|C^*|-C_{n,\tau}(\operatorname{diam}C^*)^{n-1}\alpha>0$ by the choice of $c$ in step 1.1. For each such label, steps 3.1 and 4.1-4.2 give every asserted embedding, invariance, Lagrangian, closeness and Lipschitz conclusion. All parameter choices depend on the fixed analytic data and the prescribed $\alpha,\varepsilon$; the single initial threshold is uniform for $0<\alpha<c$. No numerical value of $c$, probability claim, global regularity assertion or empirical observation is supplied. [step 1.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 4.2, F5, algebra] ∎
'''
s=s[:start]+proof+s[end:];p.write_text(s)
